@node Numbers
@section Numbers

@cindex number

It is important to distinguish between mathematical numbers, the Scheme
numbers that attempt to model them, the machine representations used to
implement the Scheme numbers, and notations used to write numbers.  This
report uses the types @dfn{number}, @dfn{complex}, @dfn{real},
@dfn{rational}, and @dfn{integer} to refer to both mathematical numbers
and Scheme numbers.

@node Numerical types
@subsection Numerical types

Mathematically, numbers are arranged into a tower of subtypes in which
each level is a subset of the level above it:

@display
number
complex number
real number
rational number
integer
@end display

For example, 3 is an integer.  Therefore 3 is also a rational, a real,
and a complex number.  The same is true of the Scheme numbers that model
3.  For Scheme numbers, these types are defined by the predicates
@code{number?}, @code{complex?}, @code{real?}, @code{rational?}, and
@code{integer?}.

There is no simple relationship between a number's type and its
rep@value{shy}re@value{shy}sen@value{shy}tation inside a computer.
Although most implementations of Scheme will offer at least two
different rep@value{shy}re@value{shy}sen@value{shy}tations of 3, these
different rep@value{shy}re@value{shy}sen@value{shy}tations denote the
same integer.

Scheme's numerical operations treat numbers as abstract data, as
in@value{shy}depen@value{shy}dent of their representation as possible.
Although an implemen@value{shy}tation of Scheme may use multiple
internal representations of numbers, this ought not to be apparent to a
casual programmer writing simple programs.

@node Exactness
@subsection Exactness
It is useful to distinguish between numbers that are represented exactly
and those that might not be.  For example, indexes into data structures
must be known exactly, as must some polynomial coefficients in a
symbolic algebra system.  On the other hand, the results of measurements
are inherently in@value{shy}ex@value{shy}act, and irrational numbers may
be approximated by rational and there@value{shy}fore
in@value{shy}ex@value{shy}act approximations.  In order to catch uses of
in@value{shy}exact numbers where exact numbers are required, Scheme
explicitly distinguishes exact from inexact numbers.  This distinction
is orthogonal to the dimension of type.

A Scheme number is @define{exact} if it was written as an exact constant
or was de@value{shy}rived from exact numbers using only exact
operations.  A number is @define{inexact} if it was written as an
inexact constant, if it was de@value{shy}rived using @define{inexact}
in@value{shy}gre@value{shy}dients, or if it was derived using
@define{inexact} operations.  Thus inexactness is a
con@value{shy}ta@value{shy}gious property of a number.  In
par@value{shy}ticular, an @define{exact complex number} has an exact
real part and an exact imaginary part; all other complex
num@value{shy}bers are @define{inexact complex numbers}.

If two implementations produce exact results for a computation that did
not involve inexact intermediate results, the two ultimate results will
be mathe@value{shy}ma@value{shy}tically equal.  This is generally not
true of computations involving in@value{shy}ex@value{shy}act numbers
since approximate methods such as floating-point arith@value{shy}metic
may be used, but it is the duty of each implementation to make the
result as close as practical to the mathematically ideal result.

Rational operations such as @code{+} should always produce exact results
when given exact arguments.  If the operation is unable to produce an
exact result, then it may either report the violation of an
implementation restriction or it may silently coerce its result to an
inexact value.  However, @code{(/ 3 4)} must not return the
mathematically incorrect value @code{0}.  @xref{Implementation
restrictions,,section 6.2.3}.

Except for @code{exact}, the operations described in this section must
generally return inexact results when given any inexact arguments.  An
operation may, however, return an exact result if it can prove that the
value of the result is unaffected by the inexactness of its arguments.
For example, multiplication of any number by an exact zero may produce
an exact zero result, even if the other argument is inexact.

Specifically, the expression @code{(* 0 +inf.0)} may return @code{0}, or
@code{+nan.0}, or re@value{shy}port that inexact numbers are not
supported, or report that non-rational real numbers are not supported,
or fail silently or noisily in other implemen@value{shy}tation-specific
ways.

@node Implementation restrictions
@subsection Implementation restrictions

Implementations of Scheme are not required to implement the whole tower
of subtypes given in @ref{Numerical types}, but they must implement a
coherent subset consistent with both the purposes of the implementation
and the spirit of the Scheme language.  For example, implementations in
which all num@value{shy}bers are real, or in which non-real
num@value{shy}bers are always inexact, or in which exact
num@value{shy}bers are always integer, are still quite useful.

Implementations may also support only a limited range of numbers of any
type, subject to the requirements of this section.  The supported range
for exact numbers of any type may be different from the supported range
for inexact numbers of that type.  For example, an implementation that
uses @acronym{IEEE} binary double-precision floating-point numbers to
represent all its in@value{shy}ex@value{shy}act real numbers may also
support a practically unbounded range of exact integers and rationals
while limiting the range of inexact reals (and there@value{shy}fore the
range of inexact integers and rationals) to the dynamic range of the
@acronym{IEEE} binary double format.  Furthermore, the gaps between the
rep@value{shy}re@value{shy}sen@value{shy}table inexact integers and
rationals are likely to be very large in such an
implemen@value{shy}tation as the limits of this range are approached.

An implementation of Scheme must support exact integers throughout the
range of numbers permitted as indexes of lists, vectors, bytevectors,
and strings or that result from computing the length of one of these.
The @code{length}, @code{vector-length}, @code{bytevector-length}, and
@code{string-length} pro@value{shy}ce@value{shy}dures must return an
exact integer, and it is an error to use anything but an exact integer
as an index.  Furthermore, any integer constant within the index range,
if expressed by an exact integer syntax, must be read as an exact
integer, regardless of any implementation restrictions that apply
out@value{shy}side this range.  Finally, the procedures listed below
will always return exact integer results provided all their arguments
are exact integers and the mathe@value{shy}ma@value{shy}tically expected
results are representable as exact integers within the implementation:

@example
-                     *
+                     abs
ceiling               denominator
exact-integer-sqrt    expt
floor                 floor/
floor-quotient        floor-remainder
gcd                   lcm
max                   min
modulo                numerator
quotient              rationalize
remainder             round
square                truncate
truncate/             truncate-quotient
truncate-remainder
@end example

It is recommended, but not required, that implementations support exact
integers and exact rationals of practically unlimited size and
precision, and to implement the above procedures and the @code{/}
procedure in such a way that they always return exact results when given
exact arguments.  If one of these procedures is unable to deliver an
exact result when given exact ar@value{shy}gu@value{shy}ments, then it
may either report a violation of an implementation
re@value{shy}stric@value{shy}tion or it may silently coerce its result
to an inexact number; such a coercion can cause an error later.
Nevertheless, implementations that do not provide exact rational numbers
should return inexact rational numbers rather than reporting an
implementation restriction.

An implementation may use floating-point and other approximate
rep@value{shy}resen@value{shy}tation strategies for inexact
numbers. This report recommends, but does not require, that
implementations that use floating-point
rep@value{shy}resen@value{shy}tations follow the @acronym{IEEE} 754
standard, and that implementations using other
rep@value{shy}resen@value{shy}ta@value{shy}tions should match or exceed
the precision achievable using these floating-point
standards@footnote{@value{refIEEE754}}.  In particular, the description
of tran@value{shy}scen@value{shy}dental functions in @acronym{IEEE}
754-2008 should be followed by such implementations, particularly with
respect to infinities and NaNs.

Although Scheme allows a variety of written notations for numbers, any
particular implementation may support only some of them.  For example,
an im@value{shy}plemen@value{shy}tation in which all numbers are real
need not support the rec@value{shy}tan@value{shy}gular and polar
notations for complex numbers.  If an implemen@value{shy}tation
en@value{shy}counters an exact numerical constant that it
can@value{shy}not represent as an exact number, then it may either
report a violation of an implementation
re@value{shy}stric@value{shy}tion or it may silently represent the
constant by an inexact number.

@node Implementation extensions
@subsection Implementation extensions

Implementations may provide more than one representation of
floating-point numbers with differing precisions.  In an implementation
which does so, an inexact result must be represented with at least as
much precision as is used to express any of the inexact arguments to
that operation.  Although it is desirable for potentially inexact
operations such as @code{sqrt} to produce ex@value{shy}act answers when
applied to exact arguments, if an exact number is oper@value{shy}ated
upon so as to produce an inexact result, then the most precise
rep@value{shy}re@value{shy}sentation available must be used.  For
example, the value of @code{(sqrt 4)} should be @code{2}, but in an
implementation that provides both single and double
pre@value{shy}cis@value{shy}ion floating point numbers it may be the
latter but must not be the former.

It is the programmer's responsibility to avoid using inexact number
objects with magnitude or significand too large to be represented in the
im@value{shy}plemen@value{shy}ta@value{shy}tion.

In addition, implementations may distinguish special numbers called
pos@value{shy}itive infinity, negative infinity, NaN, and negative zero.

Positive infinity is regarded as an inexact real (but not rational)
number that represents an indeterminate value greater than the numbers
represented by all rational numbers.  Negative infinity is regarded as
an inexact real (but not rational) number that represents an
indeterminate value less than the numbers represented by all rational
numbers.

Adding or multiplying an infinite value by any finite real value results
in an appropriately signed infinity; however, the sum of positive and
negative in@value{shy}finities is a NaN.  Positive infinity is the
reciprocal of zero, and negative in@value{shy}finity is the reciprocal
of negative zero.  The behavior of the transcendental functions is
sensitive to infinity in accordance with @acronym{IEEE} 754.

A NaN is regarded as an inexact real (but not rational) number so
in@value{shy}de@value{shy}term@value{shy}inate that it might represent
any real value, including positive or negative infinity, and might even
be greater than positive infinity or less than nega@value{shy}tive
infinity.  An implementation that does not support non-real numbers may
use NaN to represent non-real values like @nobr{@code{(sqrt -1.0)}} and
@nobr{@code{(asin 2.0)}}.

A NaN always compares false to any number, including a NaN.  An
arith@value{shy}metic operation where one operand is NaN returns NaN,
unless the im@value{shy}ple@value{shy}men@value{shy}tation can prove
that the result would be the same if the NaN were re@value{shy}placed by
any rational number.  Dividing zero by zero results in NaN
un@value{shy}less both zeros are exact.

Negative zero is an inexact real value written @code{-0.0} and is
distinct (in the sense of @code{eqv?}) from @code{0.0}.  A Scheme
implementation is not required to distinguish negative zero.  If it
does, however, the behavior of the tran@value{shy}scendental functions
is sensitive to the distinction in accordance with @acronym{IEEE} 754.
Specifically, in a Scheme implementing both complex numbers and negative
zero, the branch cut of the complex logarithm function is such that
@nobr{@code{(imag-part (log -1.0-0.0i))}} is @minus{}@U{03C0} rather
than @U{03C0}.

Furthermore, the negation of negative zero is ordinary zero and vice
versa.  This implies that the sum of two or more negative zeros is
negative, and the result of subtracting (positive) zero from a negative
zero is like@value{shy}wise neg@value{shy}a@value{shy}tive.  However,
numerical comparisons treat negative zero as equal to zero.

Note that both the real and the imaginary parts of a complex number can
be infinities, NaNs, or negative zero.

@node Syntax of numerical constants
@subsection Syntax of numerical constants

The syntax of the written representations for numbers is described
formally in Formal Constants.  Note that case is not significant in
numerical con@value{shy}stants.@c @ref{Formal syntax}.

A number can be written in binary, octal, decimal, or hexadecimal by the
use of a radix prefix.  The radix prefixes are @nobr{@hc{#b}
@U{0028}}binary@U{0029}, @nobr{@hc{#o} @U{0028}}octal@U{0029},
@nobr{@hc{#d} @U{0028}}deci@value{shy}mal@U{0029}, and @nobr{@hc{#x}
@U{0028}}hexa@value{shy}deci@value{shy}mal@U{0029}.  With no radix
prefix, a number is as@value{shy}sumed to be expressed in decimal.

A numerical constant can be specified to be either exact or inexact by a
pre@value{shy}fix.  The prefixes are @hc{#e} for exact, and @hc{#i} for
inexact.  An exactness prefix can appear before or after any radix
pre@value{shy}fix that is used.  If the written
rep@value{shy}resen@value{shy}tation of a number has no exactness
prefix, the constant is inexact if it con@value{shy}tains a decimal
point or an exponent.  Otherwise, it is exact.

In systems with inexact numbers of varying precisions it can be useful
to specify the precision of a constant.  For this purpose,
implementations may accept numerical constants written with an exponent
marker that indicates the desired precision of the inexact
representation.  If so, the letter @code{@number{s}}, @code{@number{f}},
@code{@number{d}}, or @code{@number{l}}, meaning @define{short},
@define{single}, @define{double}, or @define{long} precision,
respectively, can be used in place of @code{e}.  The default precision
has at least as much precision as double, but implementations may allow
this default to be set by the user.

@example
@number{3.14159265358979F0}
        @r{Round to single ---} @number{3.141593}
@number{0.6L0}
        @r{Extend to long ---} @number{.600000000000000}
@end example

The numbers positive infinity, negative infinity, and NaN are written
@nobr{@hc{+inf.0}}, @nobr{@hc{-inf.0}} and @nobr{@hc{+nan.0}}
respectively.  NaN may also be written @hc{-nan.0}.  The use of signs in
the written representation does not necessarily reflect the
under@value{shy}lying sign of the NaN value, if any.  Implementations
are not required to sup@value{shy}port these numbers, but if they do,
they must do so in general con@value{shy}for@value{shy}mance with
@acronym{IEEE} 754.  However, implementations are not required to
sup@value{shy}port sig@value{shy}naling NaNs, nor to provide a way to
distinguish between dif@value{shy}ferent NaNs.

There are two notations provided for non-real complex numbers: the
@define{rec@value{shy}tan@value{shy}gular notation}
@i{a}@t{+}@i{b}@t{i}, where @i{a} is the real part and @i{b} is the
imaginary part; and the @define{polar notation}
@i{r}@t{@atchar{}}@i{@U{03B8}}, where @i{r} is the magnitude and
@i{@U{03B8}} is the phase (an@value{shy}gle) in radians.  These are
related by the equation @nobr{@i{a} + @i{b}i =
@i{r}@U{2009}cos@U{2009}@i{@U{03B8}} +
(@i{r}@U{2009}sin@U{2009}@i{@U{03B8}})i}.  All of @i{a}, @i{b}, @i{r},
and @i{@U{03B8}} are real numbers.

@node Numerical operations
@subsection Numerical operations
The reader is referred to @ref{Error situations and unspecified
behavior} for a sum@value{shy}mary of the naming conventions used to
specify restrictions on the types of arguments to numerical routines.
The examples used in this section assume that any numerical constant
written using an exact notation is in@value{shy}deed represented as an
exact number.  Some examples also assume that certain numerical
constants written using an inexact notation can be
rep@value{shy}resen@value{shy}ted without loss of accuracy; the inexact
constants were chosen so that this is likely to be true in
implementations that use @acronym{IEEE} binary doubles to represent
inexact numbers.

@deffn  procedure number? obj
@deffnx procedure complex? obj
@deffnx procedure real? obj
@deffnx procedure rational? obj
@deffnx procedure integer? obj

These numerical type predicates can be applied to any kind of argument,
in@value{shy}cluding non-numbers.  They return @code{#t} if the object
is of the named type, and other@value{shy}wise they return @code{#f}.
In general, if a type predicate is true of a num@value{shy}ber then all
higher type predicates are also true of that num@value{shy}ber.
Con@value{shy}sequently, if a type predicate is false of a number, then
all lower type pred@value{shy}icates are also false of that number.

If @var{z} is a complex number, then @code{(real? @rv{z})} is true if
and only if @code{(zero? @nobr{(imag-part @rv{z})})} is true.  If
@var{x} is an inexact real number, then @code{(integer? @rv{x})} is true
if and only if @code{(= @rv{x} (round @rv{x}))}.

The numbers @nobr{@code{+inf.0}}, @nobr{@code{-inf.0}}, and
@nobr{@code{+nan.0}} are real but not rational.

@lisp
(complex? 3+4i)    @ok{#t}
(complex? 3)       @ok{#t}
(real? 3)          @ok{#t}
(real? -2.5+0i)    @ok{#t}
(real? -2.5+0.0i)  @ok{#f}
(real? #e1e10)     @ok{#t}
(real? +inf.0)     @ok{#t}
(real? +nan.0)     @ok{#t}
(rational? -inf.0) @ok{#f}
(rational? 3.5)    @ok{#t}
(rational? 6/10)   @ok{#t}
(rational? 6/3)    @ok{#t}
(integer? 3+0i)    @ok{#t}
(integer? 3.0)     @ok{#t}
(integer? 8/4)     @ok{#t}
@end lisp

@quotation
@i{Note:} The behavior of these type predicates on inexact numbers is
unreliable, since any inaccuracy might affect the result.
@end quotation

@quotation
@i{Note:} In many implementations the @code{complex?} procedure will be
the same as @code{number?}, but unusual implementations may represent
some irrational numbers exactly or may extend the number system to
sup@value{shy}port some kind of non-complex numbers.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure exact? z
@deffnx procedure inexact? z

These numerical predicates provide tests for the exactness of a
quantity.  For any Scheme number, precisely one of these predicates is
true.

@lisp
(exact? 3.0)   @ok{#f}
(exact? #e3.0) @ok{#t}
(inexact? 3.)  @ok{#t}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure exact-integer? z

Returns @code{#t} if @var{z} is both exact and an integer; otherwise
returns @code{#f}.

@lisp
(exact-integer? 32)   @ok{#t}
(exact-integer? 32.0) @ok{#f}
(exact-integer? 32/5) @ok{#f}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn {inexact library procedure} finite? z

The @code{finite?} procedure returns @code{#t} on all real numbers
except @nobr{@code{+inf.0}}, @nobr{@code{-inf.0}}, and
@nobr{@code{+nan.0}}, and on complex numbers if their real and imaginary
parts are both finite.  Otherwise it returns @code{#f}.

@lisp
(finite? 3)          @ok{#t}
(finite? +inf.0)     @ok{#f}
(finite? 3.0+inf.0i) @ok{#f}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn {inexact library procedure} infinite? z

The @code{infinite?} procedure returns @code{#t} on the real numbers
@nobr{@code{+inf.0}} and @nobr{@code{-inf.0}}, and on complex numbers if
their real or imaginary parts or both are infinite.  Otherwise it
returns @code{#f}.

@lisp
(infinite? 3)          @ok{#f}
(infinite? +inf.0)     @ok{#t}
(infinite? +nan.0)     @ok{#f}
(infinite? 3.0+inf.0i) @ok{#t}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn {inexact library procedure} nan? z

The @code{nan?} procedure returns @code{#t} on @nobr{@code{+nan.0}}, and
on complex numbers if their real or imaginary parts or both are
@nobr{@code{+nan.0}}.  Otherwise it returns @code{#f}.

@lisp
(nan? +nan.0)      @ok{#t}
(nan? 32)          @ok{#f}
(nan? +nan.0+5.0i) @ok{#t}
(nan? 1+2i)        @ok{#f}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure = @parI{z} @parII{z} @parIII{z} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure < @parI{x} @parII{x} @parIII{x} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure > @parI{x} @parII{x} @parIII{x} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure <= @parI{x} @parII{x} @parIII{x} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure >= @parI{x} @parII{x} @parIII{x} @value{defdots}

These procedures return @code{#t} if their arguments are (respectively):
equal, mono@value{shy}ton@value{shy}ically increasing,
mono@value{shy}ton@value{shy}ically decreasing,
mono@value{shy}ton@value{shy}ically non-decreasing, or
mono@value{shy}ton@value{shy}ically non-increasing, and @code{#f}
otherwise.  If any of the arguments are @nobr{@code{+nan.0}}, all the
predicates return @code{#f}.  They do not distinguish between inexact
zero and inexact negative zero.

These predicates are required to be transitive.

@quotation
@i{Note:} The implementation approach of converting all arguments to
inexact numbers if any argument is inexact is not transitive.  For
example, let @code{big} be @code{(expt 2 1000)}, and assume that
@code{big} is exact and that inexact numbers are represented by 64-bit
@acronym{IEEE} binary floating point numbers.  Then @nobr{@code{(= (-
big 1) (inexact big))}} and @nobr{@code{(= (inexact big) (+ big 1))}}
would both be true with this ap@value{shy}proach, be@value{shy}cause of
the limitations of @acronym{IEEE} representations of large integers,
whereas @nobr{@code{(= (- big 1) (+ big 1))}} is false.  Converting
in@value{shy}exact values to exact numbers that are the same (in the
sense of @code{=}) to them will avoid this problem, though special care
must be taken with infinities.
@end quotation

@quotation
@i{Note:} While it is not an error to compare inexact numbers using
these predicates, the results are unreliable because a small
in@value{shy}ac@value{shy}curacy can affect the result; this is
especially true of @code{=} and @code{zero?}.  When in doubt, consult a
numerical analyst.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure zero? z
@deffnx procedure positive? x
@deffnx procedure negative? x
@deffnx procedure odd? n
@deffnx procedure even? n

These numerical predicates test a number for a particular property,
re@value{shy}turning @code{#t} or @code{#f}.  See note above.
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure max @parI{x} @parII{x} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure min @parI{x} @parII{x} @value{defdots}

These procedures return the maximum or minimum of their arguments.

@lisp
(max 3 4)   @ok{4}    ; exact
(max 3.9 4) @ok{4.0}  ; inexact
@end lisp

@i{Note:} If any argument is inexact, then the result will also be
inexact (unless the procedure can prove that the inaccuracy is not large
enough to affect the result, which is possible only in unusual
implementations).  If @code{min} or @code{max} is used to compare
numbers of mixed exactness, and the numerical value of the result cannot
be represented as an inexact number without loss of ac@value{shy}curacy,
then the procedure may report a violation of an implementation
re@value{shy}stric@value{shy}tion.
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure + @parI{z} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure * @parI{z} @value{defdots}

These procedures return the sum or product of their arguments.

@lisp
(+ 3 4) @ok{7}
(+ 3)   @ok{3}
(+)     @ok{0}
(* 4)   @ok{4}
(*)     @ok{1}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure - z
@deffnx procedure - @parI{z} @parII{z} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure / z
@deffnx procedure / @parI{z} @parII{z} @value{defdots}

With two or more arguments, these procedures return the difference or
quo@value{shy}tient of their arguments, associating to the left.  With
one argument, how@value{shy}ever, they return the additive or
multiplicative inverse of their argument.

It is an error if any argument of @code{/} other than the first is an
exact zero.  If the first argument is an exact zero, an implementation
may return an exact zero unless one of the other arguments is a NaN.

@lisp
(- 3 4)   @ok{-1}
(- 3 4 5) @ok{-6}
(- 3)     @ok{-3}
(/ 3 4 5) @ok{3/20}
(/ 3)     @ok{1/3}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure abs x

The abs procedure returns the absolute value of its argument.

@lisp
(abs -7) @ok{7}
@end lisp

@end deffn

@deffn  procedure floor/ @parI{n} @parII{n}
@deffnx procedure floor-quotient @parI{n} @parII{n}
@deffnx procedure floor-remainder @parI{n} @parII{n}
@deffnx procedure truncate/ @parI{n} @parII{n}
@deffnx procedure truncate-quotient @parI{n} @parII{n}
@deffnx procedure truncate-remainder @parI{n} @parII{n}

These procedures implement number-theoretic (integer) division.  It is
an er@value{shy}ror if @var{n} is zero.  The procedures ending in
@code{/} return two integers; the other procedures return an integer.
All the procedures compute a quotient @varq{n} and remainder @varr{n}
such that @varI{n} = @varII{n}@varq{n} + @varr{n}.  For
each of the division operators, there are three procedures defined as
follows:

@example grammar
(@sv{operator}/ @r{@varI{n}} @r{@varII{n}})          @result{} @r{@varq{n}} @r{@varr{n}}
(@sv{operator}-quotient @r{@varI{n}} @r{@varII{n}})  @result{} @r{@varq{n}}
(@sv{operator}-remainder @r{@varI{n}} @r{@varII{n}}) @result{} @r{@varr{n}}
@end example

The remainder @varr{n} is determined the choice of integer @varq{n}:
@varr{n} = @varI{n} @minus{} @varII{n}@varq{n}.  Each set of
operators uses a different choice of @varq{n}:

@display
@t{floor      }@varq{n} = @U{23A3}@varI{n}/@varII{n}@U{23A6}
@t{truncate   }@varq{n} = truncate(@varI{n}/@varII{n})
@end display

For any of the operators, and for integers @varI{n} and @varII{n} with
@varII{n} not equal to 0,

@example grammar
(= @r{@varI{n}} (+ (* @r{@varII{n}} (@sv{operator}-quotient @r{@varI{n}} @r{@varII{n}}))
      (@sv{operator}-remainder @r{@varI{n}} @r{@varII{n}})))
                           @noadorn{@result{} @boolean{#t}}
@end example

provided all numbers involved in that computation are exact.

Examples:

@lisp
(floor/ 5 2)        @ok{2    1}
(floor/ -5 2)       @ok{3    1}
(floor/ 5 -2)       @ok{3   -1}
(floor/ -5 -2)      @ok{2   -1}
(truncate/ 5 2)     @ok{2    1}
(truncate/ -5 2)    @ok{2   -1}
(truncate/ 5 -2)    @ok{2    1}
(truncate/ -5 -2)   @ok{2   -1}
(truncate/ -5.0 -2) @ok{2.0 -1.0}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure quotient @parI{n} @parII{n}
@deffnx procedure remainder @parI{n} @parII{n}
@deffnx procedure modulo @parI{n} @parII{n}

The @code{quotient} and @code{remainder} procedures are equivalent to
@code{truncate-quotient} and @code{truncate-remainder}, respectively,
and @code{modulo} is equivalent to @code{floor-remainder}.

@quotation
@i{Note:} These procedures are provided for backward compatibility with
earlier versions of this report.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure gcd @parI{n} @value{defdots}
@deffnx procedure lcm @parI{n} @value{defdots}

These procedures return the greatest common divisor or least common
mul@value{shy}tiple of their arguments.  The result is always
non-negative.

@lisp
(gcd 32 -36)   @ok{4}
(gcd)          @ok{0}
(lcm 32 -36)   @ok{288}
(lcm 32.0 -36) @ok{288.0}  ; inexact
(lcm)          @ok{1}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure numerator q
@deffnx procedure denominator q

These procedures return the numerator or denominator of their argument;
the result is computed as if the argument was represented as a fraction
in lowest terms.  The denominator is always positive.  The denominator
of 0 is defined to be 1.

@lisp
(numerator (/ 6 4))   @ok{3}
(denominator (/ 6 4)) @ok{2}
(denominator
  (inexact (/ 6 4)))  @ok{2.0}
@end lisp

@end deffn

@deffn  procedure floor x
@deffnx procedure ceiling x
@deffnx procedure truncate x
@deffnx procedure round x

These procedures return integers.  The @code{floor} procedure returns
the largest integer not larger than @var{x}.  The @code{ceiling}
procedure returns the smallest in@value{shy}teger not smaller than
@var{x}, @code{truncate} returns the integer closest to @var{x} whose
absolute value is not larger than the absolute value of @var{x}, and
@code{round} returns the closest integer to @var{x}, rounding to even
when @var{x} is halfway between two in@value{shy}tegers.

@quotation
@i{Rationale}: The @code{round} procedure rounds to even for consistency
with the default rounding mode specified by the @acronym{IEEE} 754
@acronym{IEEE} floating-point standard.
@end quotation

@quotation
@i{Note:} If the argument to one of these procedures is inexact, then
the result will also be inexact.  If an exact value is needed, the
result can be passed to the @code{exact} procedure.  If the argument is
infinite or a NaN, then it is returned.
@end quotation

@lisp
(floor -4.3)          @ok{-5.0}
(ceiling -4.3)        @ok{-4.0}
(truncate -4.3)       @ok{-4.0}
(round -4.3)          @ok{-4.0}

(floor 3.5)           @ok{3.0}
(ceiling 3.5)         @ok{4.0}
(truncate 3.5)        @ok{3.0}
(round 3.5)           @ok{4.0}  ; inexact

(round 7/2)           @ok{4}    ; exact
(round 7)             @ok{7}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure rationalize x y

@cindex simplest rational

The @code{rationalize} procedure returns the @emph{simplest} rational
number differing from @var{x} by no more than @var{y}.  A rational
number @varI{r} is @emph{simpler} than another ra@value{shy}tional
number @varII{r} if @varI{r} = @varI{p}@varI{q} and @varII{r} =
@varII{p}@varII{q} (in lowest terms) and |@varI{p}| @leq{} |@varII{p}|
and |@varI{q}| @leq{} |@varII{q}|.  Thus @math{3/5} is simpler than
@math{4/7}.  Although not all rationals are comparable in this ordering
(consider @math{2/7} and @math{3/5}), any interval contains a rational
number that is simpler than every other rational number in that
in@value{shy}terval (the simpler @math{2/5} lies between @math{2/7} and
@math{3/5}).  Note that @math{0 = 0/1} is the simplest rational of all.

@lisp
(rationalize (exact .3) 1/10) @ok{1/3}    ; exact
(rationalize .3 1/10)         @ok{#i1/3}  ; inexact
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn  {inexact library procedure} exp z
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} log z
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} log @parI{z} @parII{z}
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} sin z
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} cos z
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} tan z
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} asin z
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} acos z
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} atan z
@deffnx {inexact library procedure} atan y x

These procedures compute the usual transcendental functions. The
@code{log} pro@value{shy}cedure computes the natural logarithm of
@var{z} (not the base ten logarithm) if a single argument is given, or
the base-@varII{z} logarithm of @varI{z} if two arguments are given.
The @code{asin}, @code{acos}, and @code{atan} procedures compute arcsine
(@inverse{sin}), arc-cosine (@inverse{cos}), and arctangent
(@inverse{tan}), respectively.  The two-argument variant of @code{atan}
computes @code{(angle (make-rectangular @rv{x} @rv{y}))} (see below),
even in implementations that don't support complex numbers.

In general, the mathematical functions log, arcsine, arc-cosine, and
arctan@value{shy}gent are multiply defined. The value of log @var{z} is
defined to be the one whose imaginary part lies in the range from
@minus{}@U{03C0} (inclusive if @code{-0.0} is dis@value{shy}tinguished,
exclusive otherwise) to @U{03C0} (inclusive).  The value of log 0 is
mathe@value{shy}matically undefined.  With log defined this way, the
values of @inverse{sin}@var{z}, @inverse{cos}@var{z}, and
@inverse{tan}@var{z} are according to the following formul@ae{}:

@display
@center @inverse{sin}@var{z} = @minus{}log(@var{i}@var{z} + √(1 @minus{} @square{@var{z}}))
@end display
@*
@display
@center @inverse{cos}@var{z} = @U{03C0}/2 @minus{} @inverse{sin}@var{z}
@end display
@*
@display
@center @inverse{tan}@var{z} = (log(1 + @var{i}@var{z}) - log(1 @minus{} @var{i}@var{z}))/(2@var{i})
@end display

However, @code{(log 0.0)} returns @code{-inf.0} (and @code{(log -0.0)}
returns @code{@number{-inf.0+}@r{@U{03C0}}@number{i}}) if the
implementation supports infinities (and @code{-0.0}).

The range of @code{(a @rv{y} @rv{x})} is as in the following table. The
asterisk (*) indicates that the entry applies to implementations that
distinguish minus zero.

