The above specification follows @cite{Common @sc{LISP}: The Language,
second edi@value{shy}tion}@footnote{@value{refCommonLispTheLanguage}},
which in turn cites @i{Principal values and branch cuts in complex
APL}@footnote{@value{refPrincipalValues}}; refer to these sources for
more detailed discussion of branch cuts, boundary conditions, and
implementation of these functions.  When it is pos@value{shy}sible,
these procedures produce a real result from a real argument.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure square z

Returns the square of @var{z}.  This is equivalent to @code{(* @rv{z}
@rv{z})}.

@lisp
(square 42)  @ok{1764}
(square 2.0) @ok{4.0}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn {inexact library procedure} sqrt z

Returns the principal square root of @var{z}.  The result will have
either a positive real part, or a zero real part and a non-negative
imaginary part.

@lisp
(sqrt 9)  @ok{3}
(sqrt -1) @ok{+i}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure exact-integer-sqrt k

Returns two non-negative exact integers @var{s} and @var{r} where
@var{k} = @square{@var{s}} + @var{r} and @var{k} @U{003C}
@square{(@var{s} + 1)}

@lisp
(exact-integer-sqrt 4) @ok{2 0}
(exact-integer-sqrt 5) @ok{2 1}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure expt @parI{z} @parII{z}

Returns @varI{z} raised to the power @varII{z}. For nonzero @varI{z},
this is

@display
@varI{z}@sup{@varII{z}} = @i{e}@sup{@varII{z}log(@varI{z})}
@end display

The value of 0@sup{@var{z}} is 1 if @code{(zero? @rv{z})}, 0 if
@code{(real-part @rv{z})} is positive, and an er@value{shy}ror
otherwise.  Similarly for 0.0@sup{@var{z}}, with inexact results.
@end deffn

@deffn  {complex library procedure} make-rectangular @parI{x} @parII{x}
@deffnx {complex library procedure} make-polar @parIII{x} @parIV{x}
@deffnx {complex library procedure} real-part z
@deffnx {complex library procedure} imag-part z
@deffnx {complex library procedure} magnitude z
@deffnx {complex library procedure} angle z

Let @varI{x}, @varII{x}, @varIII{x}, and @varIV{x} be real numbers and
@var{z} be a complex number such that

@display
@var{z} = @varI{x} + @varII{x}@i{i} = @varIII{x} @U{2219} @i{e}@sup{@i{i}@varIV{x}}
@end display

Then all of

@ifnothtml
@indentedblock
@multitable {(make-rectangular x1 x2)} {} {x__angle_}
@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}make-rectangular @r{@varI{x}} @r{@varII{x}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{z}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}make-polar @r{@varI{x}} @r{@varII{x}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{z}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}real-part @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @varI{x}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}imag-part @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @varII{x}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}magnitude @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab |@varIII{x}|

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}angle @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{x@sub{angle}}
@end multitable
@end indentedblock
@end ifnothtml
@ifhtml
@indentedblock
@multitable @columnfractions .40 .04 .56
@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}make-rectangular @r{@varI{x}} @r{@varII{x}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{z}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}make-polar @r{@varI{x}} @r{@varII{x}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{z}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}real-part @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @varI{x}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}imag-part @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @varII{x}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}magnitude @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab |@varIII{x}|

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}angle @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{x@sub{angle}}
@end multitable
@end indentedblock
@end ifhtml

are true, where @minus{}@U{03C0} @leq{} @var{x@sub{angle}} @leq{}
@U{03C0} with @var{x@sub{angle}} = @varIV{x} + 2@U{03C0}@var{n} for some
integer @var{n}.

The @code{make-polar} procedure may return an inexact complex number
even if its arguments are exact.  The @code{real-part} and
@code{imag-part} procedures may re@value{shy}turn exact real numbers
when applied to an inexact complex number if the corresponding argument
passed to @code{make-rectangular} was exact.

The @code{magnitude} procedure is the same as @code{abs} for a real
argument, but @code{abs} is in the base library, whereas
@code{magnitude} is in the optional complex library.
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure inexact z
@deffnx procedure exact z
The procedure @code{inexact} returns an inexact representation of
@var{z}.  The value re@value{shy}turned is the inexact number that is
numerically closest to the argument.  For inexact arguments, the result
is the same as the argument.  For exact complex numbers, the result is a
complex number whose real and imaginary parts are the result of applying
@code{inexact} to the real and imaginary parts of the argument,
respectively.  If an exact argument has no reasonably close inexact
equivalent (in the sense of @code{=}), then a violation of an
implementation restriction may be reported.

The procedure @code{exact} returns an exact representation of @var{z}.
The value re@value{shy}turned is the exact number that is numerically
closest to the argument.  For exact arguments, the result is the same as
the argument.  For inexact non-integral real arguments, the
implemen@value{shy}tation may return a rational
ap@value{shy}prox@value{shy}imation, or may report an
implemen@value{shy}tation violation.  For inexact complex arguments, the
result is a complex number whose real and imaginary parts are the result
of applying @code{exact} to the real and imaginary parts of the
ar@value{shy}gument, respectively.  If an inexact argument has no
reasonably close exact equivalent, (in the sense of @code{=}), then a
violation of an implementation re@value{shy}stric@value{shy}tion may be
reported.

These procedures implement the natural one-to-one correspondence
be@value{shy}tween exact and inexact integers throughout an
implementation-dependent range.  @xref{Implementation restrictions}.

@quotation
@i{Note:} These procedures were known in @value{R5RS} as
@code{exact->inexact} and @code{inexact->exact}, respectively, but they
have always accepted ar@value{shy}gu@value{shy}ments of any exactness.
The new names are clearer and shorter, as well as being compatible with
@value{R6RS}.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@node Numerical input and output
@subsection Numerical input and output

@deffn  procedure number->string z
@deffnx procedure number->string z radix

It is an error if @var{radix} is not one of 2, 8, 10, or 16.

The procedure @code{number->string} takes a number and a radix and
returns as a string an external representation of the given number in
the given radix such that

@example grammar
(let ((@letlikebind{number} @rv{number})
      (@letlikebind{radix} @rv{radix}))
  (eqv? number
        (string->number (number->string number
                                        radix)
                        radix)))
@end example

is true.  It is an error if no possible result makes this expression
true.  If omit@value{shy}ted, @var{radix} defaults to 10.

If @var{z} is inexact, the radix is 10, and the above expression can be
satisfied by a result that contains a decimal point, then the result
contains a decimal point and is expressed using the minimum number of
digits (exclusive of exponent and trailing zeroes) needed to make the
above expression true@footnote{@value{refPrintFPQuicklyAccurately}}
@footnote{@value{refReadFPAccurately}}; other@value{shy}wise the format
of the result is unspecified.

The result returned by @code{number->string} never contains an explicit
radix pre@value{shy}fix.

@quotation
@i{Note:} The error case can occur only when @var{z} is not a complex
number or is a complex number with a non-rational real or imaginary
part.
@end quotation

@quotation
@i{Rationale:} If @var{z} is an inexact number and the radix is 10, then
the above expression is normally satisfied by a result containing a
dec@value{shy}i@value{shy}mal point.  The unspecified case allows for
infinities, NaNs, and un@value{shy}usual representations.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@deffn  procedure string->number string
@deffnx procedure string->number string radix

Returns a number of the maximally precise representation expressed by
the given @var{string}.  It is an error if @var{radix} is not 2, 8, 10,
or 16.

If supplied, @var{radix} is a default radix that will be overridden if
an explicit radix prefix is present in string (e.g.@:
@code{"#o177"}). If @var{radix} is not supplied, then the
de@value{shy}fault radix is 10.  If @var{string} is not a syntactically
valid notation for a num@value{shy}ber, or would result in a number that
the implementation cannot represent, then @code{string->number} returns
@code{#f}.  An error is never signaled due to the con@value{shy}tent of
@var{string}.

@lisp
(string->number "100")    @ok{100}
(string->number "100" 16) @ok{256}
(string->number "1e2")    @ok{100.0}
@end lisp

@quotation
@i{Note:} The domain of @code{string->number} may be restricted by
im@value{shy}ple@value{shy}men@value{shy}tations in the following ways.
If all numbers supported by an implementation are real, then
@nobr{@code{string->number}} is permitted to re@value{shy}turn @code{#f}
whenever @var{string} uses the polar or rectangular notations for
complex numbers.  If all numbers are integers, then
@nobr{@code{string->number}} may return @code{#f} whenever the
fractional notation is used.  If all num@value{shy}bers are exact, then
@nobr{@code{string->number}} may return @code{#f} whenever an exponent
marker or explicit exactness aprefix is used.  If all inexact numbers
are integers, then @nobr{@code{string->number}} may return @code{#f}
when@value{shy}ever a decimal point is used.

The rules used by a particular implementation for @code{string->number}
must also be applied to @code{read} and to the routine that reads
programs, in order to maintain consistency between internal numeric
pro@value{shy}cessing, I/O, and the processing of programs.  As a
consequence, the @value{R5RS} permission to return @code{#f} when
@var{string} has an explicit radix pre@value{shy}fix has been withdrawn.
@end quotation
@end deffn
